  - algorithm engineering
  - algorithm audit
  - Rust
abstract: >-
  The first implementation of the Duan–Mao–Shu–Yin directed single-source
  shortest-path algorithm (arXiv:2602.07868, February 2026): a Python
  reference, a Rust port gated bit-exactly against it, and a variant study
  with all distances verified against Dijkstra. Two results. Measured: the
  algorithm is not faster at any storable input size — the faithful port
  runs at 38–182x of a production Dijkstra and the most engineered variant
  at 1.8–2.2x, with the prescribed degree parameter falling outside the
  paper's own validity range on every input below n = 2^4096. Audited: the
  distances are exact on every input run, but the printed pseudocode
  crashes on ordinary graphs at two lines, and the completeness clause of
  Lemma 3.7 is false as stated — a 70-vertex counterexample settles an
  incomplete vertex, and a variant that trusts the clause finishes with a
  wrong distance. The repository includes a hand proof, derived and
  independently re-derived across thirty-two recorded sessions, that a
  capped variant of the algorithm settles every vertex with its canonical
  (length, hop count) label.
